QB.add(65,[{"n":1,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with skin changes, reporting that his fingers have become thick and tight. He has developed painful ulcers on his fingertips that heal slowly. The physician notes shiny, taut skin on his face and limited neck extension. The given image shows the patient's facial features. Which of the following findings is NOT typically associated with the skin changes observed in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Bilateral symmetrical skin thickening"},{"t":"Hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas"},{"t":"Fixed flexion contractures of the fingers"},{"t":"Telangiectasia on the face and hands"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms—thickened and tight skin on the fingers, painful fingertip ulcers, shiny facial skin, and limited neck extension—are characteristic of systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given image shows the shiny facial skin with Mauskopf or mouse-like facies typically seen in scleroderma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas is NOT typically associated with systemic sclerosis. Instead, patients often exhibit early diffuse tanning without sun exposure or a \" salt-and-pepper\" appearance , particularly in darker-skinned individuals."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dermal Manifestations of Systemic Sclerosis (SSc):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early diffuse tanning without sun exposure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypopigmentation with a \"salt-and-pepper\" appearance on the scalp, upper back, and chest is common in dark-skinned individuals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leads to hair loss, reduced sweating, xerosis, and itching."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Disappearance of transverse finger creases and fixed flexion contractures causing muscle atrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Taut, shiny skin with loss of wrinkles and reduced mobility."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thinned lips, prominent perioral radial furrows, and microstomia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pinched, beak-like nose."},{"k":"img","v":8882},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"7. Telangiectasia : Dilated capillaries on face, hands, lips, and oral mucosa; correlated with microvascular disease severity. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"8. Skin Ulcerations :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic ulcers on fingertips, PIP joints, and bony prominences heal slowly and may become infected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fingertip ischemia leaves fixed digital \"pits.\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"9. Acro-Osteolysis: Resorption of terminal phalanges due to ischemia."},{"k":"img","v":8907},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"10. Calcinosis Cutis :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dystrophic calcifications in the skin and soft tissues, commonly in patients with anti-centromere antibody–positive lcSSc."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcific deposits vary in size and may ulcerate, causing pain, infections, or nerve compression."},{"k":"img","v":8912}],"qi":[8882]},{"n":2,"q":"Which of the following statements about the histopathological examination (HPE) of the images is correct? A)  B)  Image A shows a thickened dermis with collagen accumulation and loss of adipose tissue. Image B demonstrates normal glomerular architecture with no signs of injury. Image B shows vascular lesions and ischemic glomerular changes. Image A is characterized by alveolar septal fibrosis leading to gas exchange impairment.","o":[{"t":"3 and 4"},{"t":"2 and 4"},{"t":"Only 2"},{"t":"1 and 3"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) 1 and 3"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Statement 1 correctly describes the pathologic findings in the skin (Image A) , which include thickened dermis and loss of subcutaneous adipose tissue due to collagen accumulation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Statement 3 accurately reflects the findings in the kidneys (Image B) , which include vascular lesions and ischemic changes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Systemic Sclerosis:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Organ/System Pathologic Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"General Findings Widespread microangiopathy, capillary loss, and fibrosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"across organs. Early inflammation (T and B lymphocytes, macrophages) around blood vessels, progressing to non-inflammatory obliterative microangiopathy in late disease."},{"k":"img","v":8888},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Skin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Thickened dermis due to collagen accumulation and atrophic adnexal glands. Loss of subcutaneous adipose tissue replaced by collagen."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lungs NSIP pattern with interstitial fibrosis and mild"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"inflammation; less commonly UIP pattern. Alveolar septal fibrosis leads to gas exchange impairment and pulmonary hypertension (PAH). Pulmonary artery intimal thickening, often with fibrosis, and sometimes associated with veno-occlusive disease and lymphocytic bronchiolitis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gastrointestinal Muscularis propria atrophy and fibrosis, especially in the Tract lower esophagus, cause dysmotility."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Chronic reflux with esophageal ulceration, stricture, and potential Barrett’s metaplasia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Kidneys Vascular lesions in interlobular and arcuate arteries;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ischemic glomerular changes. Scleroderma renal crisis shows fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles, intimal proliferation, and thrombotic microangiopathy-like changes. systemic sclerosis kidney histopathology\" data-author=\"by Francesco Reggiani 1,2,*ORCID,Gabriella Moroni 1 andClaudio Ponticelli 3\" data-hash=\"8767\" data-license=\"Open access\" data-source=\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-4426/12/7/112 3\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"276\" src=\"https://image.prepladd er.com/notes/DCIY0GUfwXx9MQ8SEdWr1744302704.p ng\" width=\"406\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Heart Arteriolar intimal hypertrophy, myocardial fibrosis, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"contraction band necrosis. Conduction system fibrosis (especially SA node) and potential epicardial atherosclerosis. Common pericardial inflammation, sometimes with effusions."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Other Organs Synovitis of hands, progressing to fibrosis in synovium"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and tendons with friction rubs. Muscle fibrosis and atrophy, similar to polymyositis. Fibrosis in thyroid and minor salivary glands, leading to hypothyroidism and sicca syndrome. Decidual vasculopathy in placentas, associated with adverse perinatal outcomes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Statement 2 is false because Image B should show ischemic changes, not normal glomerular architecture."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Statement 4 is incorrect as it pertains to the lungs, not the skin, and does not apply to the images presented."}],"qi":[8888,8893]},{"n":3,"q":"A 52-year-old woman presents with skin changes, including thickened and tight skin on her fingers, as well as pain and ulcerations at the fingertips. On examination, she has a shiny appearance to her skin and visible telangiectasia on her face. Which of the following options includes one pulmonary and one cardiac manifestation correctly associated with her clinical features?","o":[{"t":"Tuberculosis; Congestive cardiac failure"},{"t":"Cavitation; Pericarditis"},{"t":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension; Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Non-caseating granuloma; Atrial myxoma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Pulmonary arterial hypertension; Constrictive pericarditis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's skin changes, including thickened skin and telangiectasia, suggest systemic sclerosis , which often leads to pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) and constrictive pericarditis due to vascular involvement and fibrosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Features in Systemic Sclerosis (SSc):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Major Forms of Lung Involvement: Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD) and Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH) are the primary lung issues and leading causes of SSc-related death. The coexistence of ILD and PAH worsens survival rates significantly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD) and Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH) are the primary lung issues and leading causes of SSc-related death."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The coexistence of ILD and PAH worsens survival rates significantly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD) : Evidence of ILD on HRCT in up to 65% of patients, with clinically significant ILD in 16–43%. Risk factors: Male sex, African-American race, diffuse skin involvement, severe GERD, and presence of Scl-70 autoantibodies. Progressive early decline in FVC (within 3–5 years). Symptoms: Exertional dyspnea, fatigue, dry cough, and Velcro crackles at lung bases. HRCT shows lower lobe subpleural reticular opacities and ground-glass opacities; the extent of ILD on HRCT predicts progression and mortality. PFTs show restrictive defects and reduced DLCO, often with exercise desaturation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evidence of ILD on HRCT in up to 65% of patients, with clinically significant ILD in 16–43%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk factors: Male sex, African-American race, diffuse skin involvement, severe GERD, and presence of Scl-70 autoantibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive early decline in FVC (within 3–5 years)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Exertional dyspnea, fatigue, dry cough, and Velcro crackles at lung bases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HRCT shows lower lobe subpleural reticular opacities and ground-glass opacities; the extent of ILD on HRCT predicts progression and mortality."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PFTs show restrictive defects and reduced DLCO, often with exercise desaturation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH) : Affects 8–12% of SSc patients as a late complication, often coexisting with ILD. Defined by elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure (‡20 mmHg) with normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (£15 mmHg). Risk factors: Limited cutaneous disease, older age at onset, and certain autoantibodies (e.g., anti-centromere). Symptoms progress from exertional dyspnea to angina, syncope, and right heart failure. Exam findings: Loud pulmonic component of S2, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, elevated JVP, peripheral edema. Screening: Annual echocardiography; cardiac catheterization for definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects 8–12% of SSc patients as a late complication, often coexisting with ILD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defined by elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure (‡20 mmHg) with normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (£15 mmHg)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk factors: Limited cutaneous disease, older age at onset, and certain autoantibodies (e.g., anti-centromere)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms progress from exertional dyspnea to angina, syncope, and right heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exam findings: Loud pulmonic component of S2, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, elevated JVP, peripheral edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Screening: Annual echocardiography; cardiac catheterization for definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Pulmonary Complications : Includes aspiration pneumonitis, pulmonary hemorrhage, obliterative bronchiolitis, pleural reactions, chest wall fibrosis, spontaneous pneumothorax, and increased risk of lung cancer."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Includes aspiration pneumonitis, pulmonary hemorrhage, obliterative bronchiolitis, pleural reactions, chest wall fibrosis, spontaneous pneumothorax, and increased risk of lung cancer."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD) and Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH) are the primary lung issues and leading causes of SSc-related death."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The coexistence of ILD and PAH worsens survival rates significantly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evidence of ILD on HRCT in up to 65% of patients, with clinically significant ILD in 16–43%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk factors: Male sex, African-American race, diffuse skin involvement, severe GERD, and presence of Scl-70 autoantibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive early decline in FVC (within 3–5 years)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Exertional dyspnea, fatigue, dry cough, and Velcro crackles at lung bases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HRCT shows lower lobe subpleural reticular opacities and ground-glass opacities; the extent of ILD on HRCT predicts progression and mortality."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PFTs show restrictive defects and reduced DLCO, often with exercise desaturation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects 8–12% of SSc patients as a late complication, often coexisting with ILD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Defined by elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure (‡20 mmHg) with normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (£15 mmHg)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk factors: Limited cutaneous disease, older age at onset, and certain autoantibodies (e.g., anti-centromere)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms progress from exertional dyspnea to angina, syncope, and right heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exam findings: Loud pulmonic component of S2, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, elevated JVP, peripheral edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Screening: Annual echocardiography; cardiac catheterization for definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Includes aspiration pneumonitis, pulmonary hemorrhage, obliterative bronchiolitis, pleural reactions, chest wall fibrosis, spontaneous pneumothorax, and increased risk of lung cancer."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiac Manifestations of Systemic Sclerosis (SSc):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detected in 10–50% of SSc patients, more common in diffuse cutaneous SSc (dcSSc) than limited cutaneous SSc (lcSSc)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac involvement correlates with worse outcomes in SSc."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types of Involvement : Pericardial : Includes pericarditis, pericardial effusions, constrictive pericarditis, and rarely, cardiac tamponade. Myocardial : Patchy myocardial fibrosis due to microvascular involvement, vasospasm, and ischemia-reperfusion injury leading to left ventricular systolic or diastolic dysfunction and potentially heart failure. Endocardial : Often silent but may contribute to overall dysfunction. Conduction Abnormalities : Common conduction system fibrosis, with potential heart block, premature ventricular contractions, atrial fibrillation, and supraventricular tachycardia. Myocarditis : Acute or subacute myocarditis may lead to left ventricular dysfunction, best detected by cardiac MRI or endomyocardial biopsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pericardial : Includes pericarditis, pericardial effusions, constrictive pericarditis, and rarely, cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocardial : Patchy myocardial fibrosis due to microvascular involvement, vasospasm, and ischemia-reperfusion injury leading to left ventricular systolic or diastolic dysfunction and potentially heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endocardial : Often silent but may contribute to overall dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conduction Abnormalities : Common conduction system fibrosis, with potential heart block, premature ventricular contractions, atrial fibrillation, and supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common conduction system fibrosis, with potential heart block, premature ventricular contractions, atrial fibrillation, and supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocarditis : Acute or subacute myocarditis may lead to left ventricular dysfunction, best detected by cardiac MRI or endomyocardial biopsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute or subacute myocarditis may lead to left ventricular dysfunction, best detected by cardiac MRI or endomyocardial biopsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pericardial : Includes pericarditis, pericardial effusions, constrictive pericarditis, and rarely, cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocardial : Patchy myocardial fibrosis due to microvascular involvement, vasospasm, and ischemia-reperfusion injury leading to left ventricular systolic or diastolic dysfunction and potentially heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endocardial : Often silent but may contribute to overall dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conduction Abnormalities : Common conduction system fibrosis, with potential heart block, premature ventricular contractions, atrial fibrillation, and supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common conduction system fibrosis, with potential heart block, premature ventricular contractions, atrial fibrillation, and supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocarditis : Acute or subacute myocarditis may lead to left ventricular dysfunction, best detected by cardiac MRI or endomyocardial biopsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute or subacute myocarditis may lead to left ventricular dysfunction, best detected by cardiac MRI or endomyocardial biopsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common conduction system fibrosis, with potential heart block, premature ventricular contractions, atrial fibrillation, and supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute or subacute myocarditis may lead to left ventricular dysfunction, best detected by cardiac MRI or endomyocardial biopsy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis; Congestive cardiac failure (Option A): Tuberculosis is not associated with systemic sclerosis (SSc). Congestive cardiac failure can occur due to various conditions but is not specific to SSc."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cavitation; Pericarditis (Option B): Cavitation is linked to infections, not SSc. Pericarditis can occur in SSc, but this option does not represent a key pulmonary feature."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-caseating granuloma; Atrial myxoma (Option D): Non-caseating granulomas are associated with sarcoidosis, not SSc. Atrial myxoma is unrelated to SSc, making this option incorrect."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 45-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis presents with new-onset hypertension and episodes of cold-induced finger discoloration. Which statement about her renal and vascular manifestations is false?","o":[{"t":"Scleroderma renal crisis presents with accelerated hypertension and can cause renal failure."},{"t":"Raynaud's phenomenon is the most common extracutaneous feature and can lead to ulcers."},{"t":"Anti-centromere antibodies increase the risk of scleroderma renal crisis."},{"t":"Nailfold capillaroscopy distinguishes between primary and secondary Raynaud’s."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Anti-centromere antibodies increase the risk of scleroderma renal crisis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-centromere antibodies are associated with a lower risk of scleroderma renal crisis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Renal Manifestations of Systemic Sclerosis (SSc):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Scleroderma Renal Crisis (SRC): A severe complication in <15% of SSc patients, typically within the first 4 years of disease onset. Previously had a poor survival rate (<10%), now improved with ACE inhibitor treatment. Occurs more commonly in patients with topoisomerase 1 and RNP antibodies. Anti-centromere antibodies appear to reduce risk."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A severe complication in <15% of SSc patients, typically within the first 4 years of disease onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Previously had a poor survival rate (<10%), now improved with ACE inhibitor treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs more commonly in patients with topoisomerase 1 and RNP antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-centromere antibodies appear to reduce risk."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Presentation : Commonly presents with severe accelerated hypertension (>150/90 mmHg) and oliguric acute renal failure; ~10% are normotensive but have poor outcomes. (Option A ruled out) Symptoms may include headache, blurred vision, heart failure, and pulmonary edema. Signs like tendon friction rubs, pericardial effusion, unexplained anemia, and thrombocytopenia may precede SRC. Laboratory findings: mild proteinuria, granularcasts, hematuria, thrombocytopenia, and microangiopathic hemolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Commonly presents with severe accelerated hypertension (>150/90 mmHg) and oliguric acute renal failure; ~10% are normotensive but have poor outcomes. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms may include headache, blurred vision, heart failure, and pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs like tendon friction rubs, pericardial effusion, unexplained anemia, and thrombocytopenia may precede SRC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Laboratory findings: mild proteinuria, granular casts, hematuria, thrombocytopenia, and microangiopathic hemolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asymptomatic Renal Impairment : It occurs in up to 50% of SSc patients and is often linked to other vascular issues but rarely leads to end-stage renal failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It occurs in up to 50% of SSc patients and is often linked to other vascular issues but rarely leads to end-stage renal failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A severe complication in <15% of SSc patients, typically within the first 4 years of disease onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Previously had a poor survival rate (<10%), now improved with ACE inhibitor treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs more commonly in patients with topoisomerase 1 and RNP antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-centromere antibodies appear to reduce risk."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Commonly presents with severe accelerated hypertension (>150/90 mmHg) and oliguric acute renal failure; ~10% are normotensive but have poor outcomes. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms may include headache, blurred vision, heart failure, and pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs like tendon friction rubs, pericardial effusion, unexplained anemia, and thrombocytopenia may precede SRC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Laboratory findings: mild proteinuria, granular casts, hematuria, thrombocytopenia, and microangiopathic hemolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It occurs in up to 50% of SSc patients and is often linked to other vascular issues but rarely leads to end-stage renal failure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Raynaud’s Phenomenon in Systemic Sclerosis (SSc):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common extracutaneous feature in SSc. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves episodic vasoconstriction of fingers and toes, occasionally affecting the nose and earlobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triggers : Cold exposure, emotional stress, and vibration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cold exposure, emotional stress, and vibration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Color Phases : Pallor : Initial vasoconstriction. Cyanosis : Ischemia of variable duration. Hyperemia : Reperfusion upon warming or spontaneously."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pallor : Initial vasoconstriction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyanosis : Ischemia of variable duration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperemia : Reperfusion upon warming or spontaneously."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types of Raynaud’s disease: Primary Raynaud’s Disease : No underlying cause, normal family history, no tissue necrosis or ulceration, negative ANA test, milder symptoms. Secondary Raynaud’s Phenomenon : Associated with SSc and other connective tissue diseases, often more severe with ischemic ulcers and digital loss. Occurs at older age with more severe, frequent, prolonged, and painful episodes. Complicated by ischemic digital ulcers and possible digit loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Raynaud’s Disease : No underlying cause, normal family history, no tissue necrosis or ulceration, negative ANA test, milder symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary Raynaud’s Phenomenon : Associated with SSc and other connective tissue diseases, often more severe with ischemic ulcers and digital loss. Occurs at older age with more severe, frequent, prolonged, and painful episodes. Complicated by ischemic digital ulcers and possible digit loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs at older age with more severe, frequent, prolonged, and painful episodes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complicated by ischemic digital ulcers and possible digit loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nailfold Capillaroscopy : Primary Raynaud’s : Evenly spaced parallel capillary loops. Secondary Raynaud’s : Distorted, widened, irregular capillary loops, dilated lumen, microhemorrhages, and vascular dropout. Useful for distinguishing between primary and secondary forms and for early SSc diagnosis. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Raynaud’s : Evenly spaced parallel capillary loops."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary Raynaud’s : Distorted, widened, irregular capillary loops, dilated lumen, microhemorrhages, and vascular dropout."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful for distinguishing between primary and secondary forms and for early SSc diagnosis. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cold exposure, emotional stress, and vibration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pallor : Initial vasoconstriction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyanosis : Ischemia of variable duration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperemia : Reperfusion upon warming or spontaneously."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Raynaud’s Disease : No underlying cause, normal family history, no tissue necrosis or ulceration, negative ANA test, milder symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary Raynaud’s Phenomenon : Associated with SSc and other connective tissue diseases, often more severe with ischemic ulcers and digital loss. Occurs at older age with more severe, frequent, prolonged, and painful episodes. Complicated by ischemic digital ulcers and possible digit loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs at older age with more severe, frequent, prolonged, and painful episodes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complicated by ischemic digital ulcers and possible digit loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs at older age with more severe, frequent, prolonged, and painful episodes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complicated by ischemic digital ulcers and possible digit loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Raynaud’s : Evenly spaced parallel capillary loops."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary Raynaud’s : Distorted, widened, irregular capillary loops, dilated lumen, microhemorrhages, and vascular dropout."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful for distinguishing between primary and secondary forms and for early SSc diagnosis. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8926},{"k":"img","v":8931}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with symptoms including skin thickening on her fingers, fatigue, and shortness of breath with episodes of painful swelling in her fingertips and difficulty swallowing. During her examination, a specific immunofluorescence test is performed, revealing the presence of several autoantibodies. Which of the following auto-antibodies is most likely associated with severe skin involvement and interstitial lung disease in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Centromere Proteins"},{"t":"Topoisomerase I (Scl-70)"},{"t":"RNA Polymerase III"},{"t":"U1-RNP"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Topoisomerase I (Scl-70)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms, including skin thickening, fatigue, and shortness of breath, suggest systemic sclerosis with severe skin and pulmonary involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Topoisomerase I (Scl-70) is associated with diffuse cutaneous SSc and linked to severe skin involvement, interstitial lung disease (ILD), and renal crisis."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Auto-Antibody SSc Su Prominent Characteristic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"bset"},{"k":"img","v":8937},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Topoisomerase I dcSSc (Scl-70)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Speckled pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Tendon friction rubs, digital ischemic ulcers, scleroderma, extensive skin involvement, early ILD, cardiac involvement, scleroderma renal crisis"},{"k":"img","v":8942},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Centromere lcSSc proteins"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Discrete speckled (centromere) pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"RNA polymerase dcSSc Speckled pattern III Rapidly progressive skin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"involvement, tendon friction rubs, joint contractures, GAVE, renal crisis, contemporaneous cancers; digital ulcers rare (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8949},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"U3-RNP (fibrillari dc/lc n) SSc"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":8949},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Th/To dc/lc"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SSc"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PM/Scl dc/lc Nucleolar pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SSc Calcinosis cutis, ILD, myositis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"overlap"},{"k":"img","v":8937},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Ku overlap"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Speckled pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SLE, myositis overlap"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"U1-RNP dc/lc Speckled pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SSc PAH, inflammatory arthritis, myositis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"overlap (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"U11/U12 RNP dc/lc Speckled pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SSc ILD"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dc SSc- Diffuse cutaneous SSc"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lc SSc- Limited cutaneous SSc"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 60-year-old woman presents with severe fatigue, dry mouth, difficulty swallowing, and episodes of coldness and color changes in her fingers. She reports dyspnea on exertion and has a history of gastrointestinal reflux. There is no visible skin thickening. Laboratory tests show positive anti-nuclear antibodies and anti-Scl-70 antibodies. Which condition is she most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"Limited Scleroderma"},{"t":"Diffuse Scleroderma"},{"t":"Sine Sclerosis"},{"t":"Sjögren's Syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Sine Sclerosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Given the patient's presentation (including symptoms of Raynaud’s phenomenon , dyspnea on exertion , and gastrointestinal issues such as heartburn) and positive anti-Scl-70 antibodies with no visible skin thickening , the most likely diagnosis is Sine Sclerosis ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Limited Scleroderma Diffuse Scleroderma Sine Sclerosis Most common type of scleroderma. Skin thickening over large body Presentation may include Skin thickening is usually limited to areas (fingers, hands, arms, pulmonary fibrosis or issues related fingers, hands, forearms, or face. anterior trunk, legs, and face). to the kidneys, heart, or (Option A ruled out) (Option B ruled out) gastrointestinal system, without any Internal organ damage is less likely, Significant risk of organ damage skin involvement. and life expectancy is typically (GI tract, kidneys, lungs, heart). Raynaud's phenomenon might also normal. Associated with skin dryness, be observed. Symptoms may include itching, and musculoskeletal pain. Antinuclear antibodies may be gastrointestinal (GI) issues Life-threatening when lungs or detected, such as anti-Scl-70, ACA, (especially heartburn), severe heart are severely involved. or anti-RNA polymerase I, II, or III. Raynaud’s phenomenon, and Acute, severe high blood pressure musculoskeletal pain. can lead to kidney damage. A small subset of patients can develop life-threatening pulmonary hypertension. Subtype - CREST Syndrome : Defined by five main features: Calcinosis : Calcium deposits under the skin or in tissues. Raynaud’s Phenomenon : Color changes, cold, numbness, or tingling in fingers/toes. Esophageal Dysmotility : Causes heartburn. Sclerodactyly : Thickened skin on fingers. Telangiectasias : Red spots from enlarged blood vessels on fingers, face, or other areas."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sjögren's Syndrome (Option D) is an autoimmune disorder primarily affecting moisture-producing glands, leading to dry mouth and eyes. While it could explain some symptoms, it does not account for the presence of Raynaud's phenomenon or systemic scleroderma features."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 25-year-old female presents with episodes of sequential color changes in her fingers (white, blue, and red) triggered by cold exposure. She has no underlying conditions, normal nail fold capillaroscopy, and symmetric involvement. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis","o":[{"t":"Secondary Raynaud's Due To Scleroderma"},{"t":"Primary Raynaud's Phenomenon"},{"t":"Acrocyanosis"},{"t":"Secondary Raynaud's Due To SLE"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Primary Raynaud's Phenomenon"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient has Primary Raynaud's phenomenon because:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Young female (age 20-40 years, female: male ratio 5:1)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic triphasic color changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No underlying condition"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal nail fold capillaroscopy showing regular hairpin loops"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symmetric involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cold trigger"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Raynaud's Phenomenon:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Primary Raynaud's:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No underlying disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal nail fold capillaries"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Accounts for >50% of cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Better prognosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Improves in 15%, progresses in 30%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Secondary Raynaud's:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common causes include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Connective tissue diseases (especially scleroderma: 80-90%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arterial occlusive diseases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood dyscrasias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs (ergot, beta-blockers, bleomycin)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occupational (vibrating tools)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sequential color changes: White (Ischemia) fi Blue (Deoxygenation) fi Red (Reperfusion)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triggered by cold exposure or emotional stress"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It may affect fingers, toes, nose, ears"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can be associated with pain, numbness, or paresthesias"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conservative: Warm clothing, avoid cold temperatures, smoking cessation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medications for severe cases: Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers Alpha-1 Blockers- Prazosin Phosphodiesterase-5 Inhibitors Topical Nitroglycerin Digital sympathectomy in refractory cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alpha-1 Blockers- Prazosin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phosphodiesterase-5 Inhibitors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Topical Nitroglycerin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digital sympathectomy in refractory cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alpha-1 Blockers- Prazosin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phosphodiesterase-5 Inhibitors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Topical Nitroglycerin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digital sympathectomy in refractory cases"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary Raynaud's Due To Scleroderma (Option A): There are no signs of scleroderma-like skin tightening, sclerodactyly, or internal organ involvement, and is associated with digital ulcers and tissue loss"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acrocyanosis (Option C): Acrocyanosis is persistent rather than episodic, does not show triphasic color changes, discoloration extends proximally from digits, and no blanching occurs."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary Raynaud's Due To SLE (Option D): Only about 20% of SLE patients develop Raynaud's, other systemic symptoms of SLE would be present, and may have associated antibodies."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 42-year-old female with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis presents with severe headache, blurred vision, and blood pressure of 180/100 mmHg. Laboratory findings show creatinine of 2.8 mg/l, without thrombocytopenia or fragmented RBCs in peripheral smear. She has recently started on prednisolone 20mg daily for severe skin involvement. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":[{"t":"Start calcium channel blockers and taper steroids"},{"t":"Immediate initiation of short-acting ACE inhibitors"},{"t":"Start plasmapheresis for suspected TTP"},{"t":"Increase steroid dose to control inflammation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Immediate initiation of short-acting ace inhibitors"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Short-acting ACE inhibitors are the first-line treatment for scleroderma renal crisis. This is a medical emergency requiring:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immediate hospitalization"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prompt initiation of ACE inhibitors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapid blood pressure normalization"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment has dramatically improved survival from <10% to >60%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Scleroderma Renal Crisis:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Risk factors Early disease stage (<4 years)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diffuse/progressive skin involvement Anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies African-American race Male sex Glucocorticoid use"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical presentation Accelerated hypertension (>150/90 mmHg)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Progressive oliguric renal insufficiency Can be normotensive in 10% cases (worse prognosis) Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia Thrombocytopenia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Warning signs Palpable tendon friction rubs"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pericardial effusion New unexplained anemia Thrombocytopenia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management First-line: short-acting ACE inhibitors"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"If needed: Angiotensin II receptor blockers Calcium channel blockers Endothelin-1 receptor blockers Up to 2/3 require dialysis 30-50% can discontinue dialysis eventually Consider a kidney transplant after 2 years of dialysis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevention Daily BP monitoring in high-risk patients"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Avoid nephrotoxic drugs Limit glucocorticoids to <10 mg/day Immediate reporting of BP changes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Start calcium channel blockers and taper steroids (Option A) : While useful for Raynaud's phenomenon, calcium channel blockers are not the first-line treatment for scleroderma renal crisis. They may be added if ACE inhibitors are inadequate for blood pressure control."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Start plasmapheresis for suspected TTP (Option C) : While the presentation might mimic TTP with microangiopathic hemolysis and thrombocytopenia, this is characteristic of Scleroderma renal crisis. Misdiagnosing as TTP can delay appropriate treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increase steroid dose to control inflammation (Option D): This would worsen the condition as glucocorticoids are a risk factor for scleroderma renal crisis. high-dose steroids (>10 mg/day) should be avoided in high-risk patients."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 42-year-old female with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis presents with worsening dyspnea and reduced lung function tests showing a restrictive pattern. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment choice?","o":[{"t":"High-dose oral glucocorticoids"},{"t":"Cyclophosphamide"},{"t":"Hydroxychloroquine"},{"t":"Thalidomide"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Cyclophosphamide"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclophosphamide has been shown to reduce the progression of SSC-associated interstitial lung disease (ILD)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can stabilize and provide modest improvement in pulmonary function, HRCT findings, respiratory symptoms, and skin induration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"While it has potential toxicity, it remains an effective option for progressive SSC-ILD."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of systemic sclerosis:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disease-modifying agents Cyclophosphamide - for ILD"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mycophenolate mofetil - for skin and lung involvement Tocilizumab - for skin and lung involvement Nintedanib - for SSC-ILD"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vascular therapy Calcium channel blockers - for Raynaud's"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Angiotensin II receptor blockers Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors Bosentan - for digital ulcers"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Novel therapies Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) for"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"severe cases Rituximab"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-dose oral glucocorticoids (Option A) : While glucocorticoids may help with early inflammatory symptoms, they do not influence the progression of skin or internal organ involvement. Moreover, their use is associated with an increased risk of scleroderma renal crisis, making them potentially dangerous."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hydroxychloroquine (Option C): current literature does not well support the use of hydroxychloroquine in SSC therapy. It lacks evidence for efficacy in treating SSC-associated ILD."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thalidomide (Option D) : the use of thalidomide is not a standard treatment option for SSC-ILD."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with dry eyes and a dry mouth that has been persistent for the past 8 months. She also reports joint stiffness, fatigue, and recent dental caries. Histology reveals lymphocytic infiltration in the salivary glands. Which of the following factors is most directly implicated in the pathogenesis of her condition?","o":[{"t":"CD8+ T cell infiltration in exocrine glands"},{"t":"Hypergammaglobulinemia with elevated B-cell activation"},{"t":"Mutations in the CFTR gene"},{"t":"Eosinophilic infiltration in minor salivary glands"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Hypergammaglobulinemia with elevated B-cell activation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sjogren Syndrome pathogenesis involves hyperactivity of B-cells, resulting in hypergammaglobulinemia and the production of autoantibodies (e.g., anti-Ro and anti-La)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This autoimmune activity leads to chronic lymphocytic infiltration in the exocrine glands, causing dryness and inflammation of the lacrimal and salivary glandsn."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sjogren Syndrome is an autoimmune disorder primarily affecting the exocrine glands."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pathogenesis involves lymphocytic infiltration (mainly T and B cells) into exocrine glands, especially the salivary and lacrimal glands."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A complex interaction of genetic predisposition, environmental factors, and abnormal immune response triggers this infiltration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B-cell hyperactivity: Characterized by hypergammaglobulinemia and autoantibody production (e.g., anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Epithelial cell role: Exocrine gland epithelial cells may express autoantigens (Ro, La), produce pro-inflammatory cytokines, and express Toll-like receptors, facilitating immune activationn."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic predisposition: Certain HLA alleles (e.g., HLA-DQA1*0501) and immune-regulatory genes (IRF5, STAT4) contribute to increased susceptibility."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"img","v":8958},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The hallmark symptoms are xerostomia (dry mouth) and keratoconjunctivitis sicca (dry eyes). Other symptoms include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral symptoms: Dry mouth, difficulty swallowing, increased dental caries, and parotid gland enlargement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ocular symptoms: Gritty sensation, burning, decreased tear production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic involvement: Fatigue, joint pain, Raynaud’s phenomenon, and increased risk of lymphoma, particularly marginal zone B-cell lymphoman."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CD8+ T cell infiltration in exocrine glands (Option A): CD4+ T cells predominantly infiltrate the glands in Sjogren Syndrome, not CD8+ T cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mutations in CFTR gene (Option C): CFTR gene mutations are associated with cystic fibrosis, not Sjogren Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eosinophilic infiltration in minor salivary glands (Option D): Sjogren Syndrome typically shows lymphocytic rather than eosinophilic infiltration."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 47-year-old female presents with a history of persistent dry mouth and a gritty sensation in her eyes. Her clinical evaluation is significant for dry mucous membranes and parotid gland swelling. A labial salivary gland biopsy is performed, and the histopathological examination reveals inflammatory infiltration. Based on the histopathological findings in the provided image, which of the following features would most specifically support a diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Presence of multiple non-caseating granulomas"},{"t":"Lymphocytic foci surrounding ducts with a focus score ‡ 1 per 4 mm² area"},{"t":"Diffuse infiltration predominantly by neutrophils"},{"t":"Eosinophilic infiltration with periductal fibrosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Lymphocytic foci surrounding ducts with a focus score ‡ 1 per 4 mm² area"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most characteristic histologic feature of Sjogren Syndrome is the presence of lymphocytic infiltration in the minor salivary glands, quantified by a focus score. This infiltration is organized into lymphocytic foci surrounding ducts and acini."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unlike other glandular conditions, the presence of these lymphocytic clusters, in patients with sicca symptoms, is highly suggestive of Sjögren syndrome, distinguishing it from conditions like sarcoidosis or IgG4-related disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The focus score —a measure of the number of lymphocyte clusters per 4 mm² area—helps in diagnosing Sjögren syndrome when it meets or exceeds 1."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Histopathology of Sjogren Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocytic focus formation: Primarily CD4+ T cells and B cells, with occasional ectopic germinal centers."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Epithelial cell involvement: Glandular epithelial cells may undergo apoptosis, and they often express inflammatory markers that perpetuate immune responsen(ECG)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic germinal centers: These structures promote autoantibody production and enhance local autoimmune activity."},{"k":"img","v":8962},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig: Focal lymphoid infiltration in the minor salivary gland associated with Sjögren syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Presence of multiple non-caseating granulomas (Option A): Non-caseating granulomas are not characteristic of Sjögren syndrome; they are more commonly associated with conditions like sarcoidosis, which may also present with salivary gland enlargement but lack the lymphocytic ductal infiltration typical of Sjögren syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diffuse infiltration predominantly by neutrophils (Option C): Sjögren syndrome is primarily a lymphocyte-driven autoimmune disorder. Neutrophilic infiltration is indicative of an acute inflammatory or infectious process, not chronic autoimmune conditions like Sjögren syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eosinophilic infiltration with periductal fibrosis (Option D): Eosinophilic infiltration is more commonly seen in allergic reactions or certain parasitic infections. While fibrosis can be present in Sjögren syndrome, the predominant cellular infiltrate is lymphocytic, not eosinophilic."}],"qi":[8899]},{"n":12,"q":"A patient diagnosed with Sjogren Syndrome reports persistent dry eyes, despite using artificial tears. Which of the following treatments would be considered next to alleviate the ocular symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Oral corticosteroids"},{"t":"Antihistamines"},{"t":"Topical cyclosporine eye drops"},{"t":"NSAIDs"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) Topical cyclosporine eye drops"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: Topical cyclosporine eye drops are used to reduce inflammation and increase tear production in patients with Sjogren Syndrome. This treatment is effective for patients who do not respond adequately to artificial tearsn."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Sjogren Syndrome: Diagnosis involves clinical features supported by specific tests."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dry eyes and/or dry mouth"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Major salivary gland enlargement, fatigue, Raynaud’s phenomenon, arthralgias/arthritis, palpable purpura/urticarial lesions, renal tubular acidosis/MPGN, ILD/small airways disease, autoimmune cholangitis, peripheral neuropathy, MS-like lesions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tests:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Any of the following is present:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unstimulated whole saliva flow rate £0.1 mL/min"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Schirmer’s test £5 mm/5 min on at least one eye"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ocular staining score ‡5 (or van Bijsterveld’s score ‡4) on at least one eye"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"And, any of the following:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum antibodies - Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Minor salivary gland biopsy (A labial biopsy showing lymphocytic infiltration with a focus score ‡1)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment: Treatment is largely symptomatic and involves:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ocular symptoms: Artificial tears and, if needed, topical cyclosporine for inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Xerostomia: Regular sips of water and pilocarpine or cevimeline to stimulate saliva."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systemic disease: Hydroxychloroquine for mild symptoms and in severe cases, immunosuppressants like rituximabn."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oral corticosteroids (Option A) : While corticosteroids can reduce systemic inflammation, they are not typically used for local ocular symptoms in Sjogren Syndrome, especially as a next-line therapy for dry eyes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antihistamines (Option B) : Antihistamines are more likely to worsen dryness as they reduce tear production, so they are inappropriate for treating dry eyes in Sjogren Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NSAIDs (Option D) : NSAIDs do not directly address the underlying inflammatory mechanisms of dry eyes in Sjogren Syndrome and are not typically used for this indication."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"8962":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8899":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8931":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8937":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8907":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8942":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8958":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8912":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8882":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8949":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8888":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8893":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8926":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});